---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    provenance={meta}
    route={['Spain', 'Catalonia', 'Barcelona']}
    label="Itinerary · One Day"
    title="One Day in Barcelona:"
    titleEmphasis="From the Hill to the Sea"
    standfirst="Two tickets with clocks attached decide the shape of this day. Everything after them is a matter of walking downhill — and of being honest about the four or five famous things that will not fit."
    stats={[
      { num: '2', label: 'Timed Tickets' },
      { num: '5', label: 'Route Stages' },
      { num: '~8 km', label: 'On Foot' },
      { num: '~11 h', label: 'Gate to Water' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Barcelona will not be finished in a day, and the useful response to
          that is not to move faster. It is to stop treating the city as a list.
          What defeats a single day here is not the number of things worth
          seeing — most large cities have more than a day's worth — but the fact
          that Barcelona's are distributed across four grounds that do not sit
          near one another. The great park is on the northern hillside. The
          basilica stands alone in a nineteenth-century grid a mile inland. The
          medieval city is a dense knot down by the old shore. The water is
          behind that again. An itinerary that treats those as interchangeable
          stops spends the day climbing and re-descending one slope, and arrives
          at evening having seen five entrances and one city.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is a shape hidden in that geography and it works in only one
          direction. Barcelona rises from the sea to the ridge of Collserola:
          gently through the old city, steadily through the Eixample, sharply
          into Gràcia and the hills behind. Every gradient is therefore a
          resource rather than an obstacle, provided you spend it in the right
          order — begin at the top in the cool of the morning and let the day
          fall towards the water. The reverse is the same distance and roughly
          twice the work, and it puts the two hardest-to-book sights at the end
          of a day on which you are already late.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other governing fact is administrative rather than topographic.
          Exactly two things on this route sell entry by the clock, and they are
          the two things nobody wants to miss. Park Güell admits by time band;
          the Sagrada Família admits by time band and does not forgive a late
          arrival. Fix those two and the day has a spine. Everything after
          them — the grid, the Gothic Quarter, El Born, the harbour — is
          unticketed, unscheduled and improves as the light goes. That is the
          whole argument of what follows: buy the morning, then walk down into
          an afternoon that costs nothing and cannot sell out. The longer
          versions of this city are recorded elsewhere on this site, as
          <a href="/7-days-in-barcelona/">a week read through Gaudí</a> and in
          the assessment of
          <a href="/best-areas-to-stay-in-barcelona/">where to sleep between
          hill and sea</a>. This is not a compression of either. At one day, the
          cuts are the content.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/park-guell-terrace-over-the-city.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/park-guell-terrace-over-the-city-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/park-guell-terrace-over-the-city-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/park-guell-terrace-over-the-city-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/park-guell-terrace-over-the-city-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/park-guell-terrace-over-the-city.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The wide sandy terrace of the Nature Square in Park Güell, Barcelona, framed by two tall date palms, with several dozen visitors scattered across it, a tiled pinnacle and the roofs of the entrance pavilions below, the city grid running away to the Sagrada Família and its cranes on the left, and the Mediterranean along the horizon under a pale blue sky."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="The entire route in one frame, read from the top down: the terrace you start on, the basilica standing out of the grid on the left, and the flat grey line of the sea closing the horizon. Everything in this article happens between the palms and that line."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="who-for" rule={false}>Who this day is for</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        A first visit, with one full day rather than a cruise call or a morning
        between flights. It assumes a traveller who will walk seven or eight
        kilometres without resenting it, who will use the metro and a bus
        without needing to be persuaded, and who has booked two tickets before
        leaving home. It assumes that urban history and architecture are the
        point, and that shopping and the beach are not. It does not assume any
        Catalan, a car, a guide or a tour.
      </p>
      <p>
        It does assume mobility, and that is worth stating plainly rather than
        hiding at the bottom of the page. Park Güell's own visitor information
        describes irregular paths and steep ramps that make the site difficult
        for people with reduced mobility; the approach from either metro
        station is a twenty-minute climb; the Gothic Quarter is uneven stone
        throughout. Anyone travelling with a significant mobility constraint
        should treat this as a sketch rather than a plan — take the shuttle bus
        described below rather than the walk up, budget for taxis between the
        Eixample and the old city, and accept that the Gothic lanes and the
        basilica's towers will not both be possible. The rest of the day
        survives that adaptation. The climb does not.
      </p>
    </div>

